{"records":[{"id":"6msmhr9vrs","title":"Inflammasome-mediated glucose limitation induces antibiotic tolerance in Staphylococcus aureus ","description":"Interactions with the host immune system alters S. aureus response to therapeutic antibiotics. We find that S. aureus α-toxin, a pore-forming toxin, interacts with macrophages to alter the microenvironment of the pathogen and influence its susceptibility to antibiotics. α-toxin-mediated activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome induces antibiotic tolerance through increased glycolysis in the host cells, resulting in glucose limitation and ATP depletion in S. aureus. Additionally, inhibition of NLRP3 activation improves antibiotic efficacy in vitro and in vivo. Our findings identify interactions between S. aureus and the host that result in metabolic crosstalk that can determine the outcome of antimicrobial therapy. This data contains GraphPad Prism files containing data from the main and supplemental figures of J.E.Beam. et al., 2023.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-03T02:50:51.098Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6msmhr9vrs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6msmhr9vrs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nikki","last_name":"Wagner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jenna","last_name":"Beam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kuan-Yi","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Parsons","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Conlon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Conlon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Antibiotics","Inflammasome","Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dkfkfnyzs","title":"Prevalence data of diarrheagenic Escherichia coli in the fecal pellets of wild rodents using culture methods and PCR assay","description":"The Escherichia coli and Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, bacteria in the fecal samples were detected by culture and molecular method. The rodent fecal samples were first cultured in a non-selective buffered peptone water (BPW) and then the enrichment broth containing the samples was streaked with a loop onto the E. coli-selective eosin methylene blue agar (EMB) media and Shiga toxin-producing E. coli-selective cefixime tellurite sorbitol MacConkey agar (CT-SMAC) media and incubated. The plates were examined for colony forming units (CFU) and sub-cultivated was conducted on EMB so that pure colonies could be collected. The E. coli colonies produced metallic sheen color produced on EMB agar media. Sorbitol negative colonies (white or off white color) were detected onto CT-SMAC agar (Fig. S1). We select colorless colony as E. coli O157:H7 positive colonies seem to be STEC-positive on culture media. Finally the STEC E. coli was detected based on morphology, PCR band, and sequence similarity analysis. The E. coli and Shiga toxin-producing E. coli sequences were compared for similarity with bacteria deposited in GenBank using NCBI BLAST, which is available at http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/.\n\nThe bacterial colonies on the selective media that had been identified by their morphology were re-identified by PCR using molecular markers. A PCR amplification of hypervariable regions (HVR) the bacterial 16S rRNA gene was performed to confirm whether the colonies on the EMB media belonged to E. coli. We randomly selected a single colony from each of the 26 EMB agar plates and after PCR amplification with the HVR primer set, the target bands for the 16S rRNA gene were found in all 26 single colonies (Fig. S2). Thirteen PCR bands were then sequenced and deposited to the GenBank database.\n\nA PCR amplification of the Shiga toxin genes (stx1 and stx2) was performed to confirm whether the colonies on the CT-SMAC media belonged to Shiga toxin-producing E. coli. We randomly selected one or more single colonies from each of the 18 CT-SMAC agar plates. Two single colonies from the CT-SMAC plate for MuRaNo-1, three single colonies from MuApSy-3, and a single colony from the others were selected, which resulted in 21 colonies. The PCR amplification using the Stx1 primer set produced bands for the Shiga toxin gene from all of the 21 single colonies (Fig S3). We then sequenced 13 PCR bands from the 21 colonies. The target PCR bands of 5 E. coli with the stx2 gene (Fig S4) were amplified and sequenced, deposited to the GenBank database.\nRaw data sequence of 16S gene with their colony ID and GenBank accession No. is provided as fasta text file. In addition, similarly, Shiga toxin gene sequences (stx1 and stx2) of raw data set are described with colony ID and GenBank accession No.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-26T19:44:51.112Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dkfkfnyzs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dkfkfnyzs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Md Mafizur","last_name":"Rahman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanjin ","last_name":"Lim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heuk ","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jay","last_name":"Cho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yung ","last_name":"Park","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"km23bwyny6","title":"Identification of required host factors for SARS-CoV-2 infection in human cells, Daniloski et al","description":"Supplementary Tables S1 to S6 in MS Excel format for \"Identification of required host factors for SARS-CoV-2 infection in human cells\" by Daniloski*, Jordan* et al. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-21T14:00:41.151Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/km23bwyny6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/km23bwyny6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zharko","last_name":"Daniloski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tristan","last_name":"Jordan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hans-Hermann","last_name":"Wessels","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daisy","last_name":"Hoagland","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silva","last_name":"Kasela","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mateusz","last_name":"Legut","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silas","last_name":"Maniatis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eleni","last_name":"Mimitou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Evan","last_name":"Geller","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Oded","last_name":"Danziger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brad","last_name":"Rosenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hemali","last_name":"Phatnani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Smibert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tuuli","last_name":"Lappalainen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"tenOever","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Neville","last_name":"Sanjana","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z2h3dk3n9f","title":"Metabolomics data 2024","description":"The metabolomics data was provided by the Biomarker Technologies Corporation before, involving in the interaction among Toxoplasma gondii, host Vero cells and herbal medicines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-02T08:30:58.804Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z2h3dk3n9f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z2h3dk3n9f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"先生","last_name":"李","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5mjjfcdc2","title":"Cell-specific targeting by Clostridium perfringens β-toxin unraveled: the role of CD31 as the toxin receptor.","description":"Data set for publication in Cell Host Microbe, Bruggisser et al: Cell-specific targeting by Clostridium perfringens β-toxin unraveled: the role of CD31 as the toxin receptor.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-04T07:00:58.559Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5mjjfcdc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5mjjfcdc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Horst","last_name":"Posthaus","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julia","last_name":"Bruggisser","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Receptor","Pore Forming Protein","Clostridium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fv8bz3tg8p","title":"Data for: Anti-inflammatory Potential of South African Medicinal Plants used in the Treatment of Sexually Transmitted Infections","description":"The dataset represents biological assays, conducted to determine the inhibitory activity of extracts from 12 South African medicinal plants on selected inflammatory enzymes, as well as their ability to reduce nitric oxide production, in response to inflammatory stimulation with lipopolysaccharide (LPS) in macrophage cells. \n\nThe viability of macrophage cells and confirmation of cytotoxicity status of active extracts was done on Vero cells using the MTT assay.\n\nResults of antioxidant scavenging capacity of extracts are also presented.\n\nNMR spectroscopic data are presented as mestronova files while the amount of phenolic and flavonoid contents in the extracts were determined from a calibration curve of gallic acid and quercetin as shown in the data set.\n\nAll experiments were carried out according to standard protocols as described in the full article.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-08T16:34:16.929Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fv8bz3tg8p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fv8bz3tg8p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fatimah","last_name":"Lawal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thilivhali Emmanuel","last_name":"Tshikalange","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lyndy","last_name":"McGaw","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Salmon Alani","last_name":"Adebayo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karl Egil","last_name":"Malterud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanah","last_name":"Nkadimeng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Johanna","last_name":"Bapela","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy","Phytochemical Analysis","Enzymatic Activity","Antioxidant Capacity","Cytotoxicity Assay"],"journal_issn":["02546299"]},{"id":"5w9pvndgrf","title":"Ca2+ is an intracellular danger signal promoting STING activation","description":"Calcium release in host cells is essential for viral infection and survival, and mutations disrupting calcium homeostasis are associated with chronic viral infections or autoimmune diseases in patients, yet the biological significance of Ca2+ signaling on STING activation remains unclear. Here we show that cytosolic Ca2+ is sensed by host cells as an immediate-early danger signal. Viral infection-activated Rho GTPases and phospholipase C-β induce ITPR-mediated, while STING translocation induces TRPV2-mediated ER Ca2+ release, both leading to store-operated Ca2+ entry through ORAI channels. Ca2+ influx activates calmodulin (CaM)-dependent protein kinases CaMKK and CaMK1D. Activated CaMK1D phosphorylates STING allowing for TBK1 trans-autophosphorylation. Inhibiting Ca2+ signaling or mutating CaMK1D-phosphorylating STING sites impairs STING activation. Ca2+ signaling is also required for STING-mediated autophagy and lysosome biogenesis, inhibited by CaMKK or CaMK inhibitors, or by deleting CaMKK1/2 or CaMK1D. These results support a two-signal mode for STING activation in which the Ca2+-CaM-CaMKK-CaMK cascade is indispensable.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-15T15:35:17.628Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5w9pvndgrf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5w9pvndgrf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nanhai","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Han","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xurui","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanyuan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanhao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yilin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Run","last_name":"Fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenguang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luhua","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Phosphorylation","Autophagy","Innate Immunity","Calcium","Phospholipase C","cGAS-STING Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6kntht4s2z","title":"Cancer Cell Cytotoxicity and Viral DNA Replication Inhibition Data ","description":"Chemical Compounds Characterization data:  NMR and HRMS\nNMR raw data \nMTT assay description and representative graphs \nSRB assay in 60 cancer cell lines for selected compounds\nAnti-viral screening (including cytotoxicity in host cells)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-26T10:16:39.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6kntht4s2z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6kntht4s2z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vladislav","last_name":"Litosh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry","Mass Spectrometry","Nuclear Magnetic Resonance","Bioassay"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":144892,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":191,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":144892,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":144063,"restricted":73,"embargoed":756},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of Chi